  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Behavior",
    "lesson_core_move": "Identify low-pass behavior in $H(j\\omega)$ by comparing its magnitude at low and high frequencies and interpreting strong low-frequency passage with high-frequency attenuation as the system preserving slow sinusoidal components while suppressing rapid ones.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Low-Pass Behavior",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A system has low-pass behavior when its magnitude response is larger at low frequencies than at high frequencies. The cue is the comparison of $|H(j\\omega)|$ across frequency, not a phase pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| > |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In the common base case, the low-frequency gain is close to $1$ while the high-frequency gain is much smaller. That means the same sinusoid is passed with little attenuation when it is slow and with much more attenuation when it is fast.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| \\approx 1,\\quad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| \\ll 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For a sinusoidal input at frequency $\\omega$, the output amplitude is the input amplitude multiplied by $|H(j\\omega)|$. So the system keeps slow sinusoidal components prominent and suppresses rapid ones.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}} = A_{\\text{in}}\\,|H(j\\omega)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the low-pass rule you will reuse: look at the magnitude response, compare low and high frequency gain, and classify the system by whether it preserves slow components more strongly than fast ones.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing Strong Low-Frequency Passage from |$H(j\\omega)$|",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system has $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| = 0.95$ at a small frequency and $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| = 0.08$ at a much larger frequency. What does that tell you about how the system treats slow and rapid sinusoidal components?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the comparison of the two magnitudes, not the phase. Read the low-frequency value and the high-frequency value as gains for slow and rapid sinusoidal components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| = 0.95,\\quad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| = 0.08",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $0.95$ is close to $1$ and $0.08$ is much smaller, the low-frequency component is passed with little attenuation while the high-frequency component is strongly reduced.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}} = A_{\\text{in}}\\,|H(j\\omega)|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the slow sinusoidal part stays prominent in the output, while the rapid sinusoidal part is suppressed. That is exactly the low-pass pattern.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Recognizing High-Frequency Attenuation from |$H(j\\omega)$|",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "At $\\omega = 40$, the magnitude response is $|H(j40)| = 0.05$. What happens to a sinusoidal input at that frequency?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the small magnitude at a high frequency. Read $|H(j40)| = 0.05$ as the gain applied to a rapid sinusoidal component.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j40)| = 0.05",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That means the output keeps the same frequency $40$ but its amplitude is only $0.05$ of the input amplitude. The frequency stays the same; the size shrinks.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "A_{\\text{out}} = 0.05\\,A_{\\text{in}}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the fast component is strongly attenuated. That is the high-frequency side of low-pass behavior.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Comparing Low- and High-Frequency Magnitudes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A system has $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| = 1.1$ and $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| = 0.2$. Which frequency is passed more strongly, and what filter behavior does that imply?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the comparison itself: read $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})|$ and $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})|$ side by side, then decide which one is larger.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| = 1.1,\\quad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})| = 0.2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Since $1.1 > 0.2$, the low-frequency component is passed more strongly. The larger low-frequency magnitude means the slow sinusoidal part survives much better than the fast one.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "|H(j\\omega_{\\text{low}})| > |H(j\\omega_{\\text{high}})|",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That contrast identifies low-pass behavior: the system preserves slow components and suppresses rapid components.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Low-Pass Behavior",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Interpreting Low-Pass Behavior for Sinusoidal Components",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "An input is $x(t) = \\cos(\\omega_{\\text{slow}} t) + \\cos(\\omega_{\\text{fast}} t)$ with $\\omega_{\\text{slow}}$ small and $\\omega_{\\text{fast}}$ large. If $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{slow}})| \\approx 1$ and $|H(j\\omega_{\\text{fast}})| \\approx 0.1$, what does the output look like component by component?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is to separate the input into its slow and fast sinusoidal parts, then attach the corresponding gain to each one.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t) = \\cos(\\omega_{\\text{slow}} t) + \\cos(\\omega_{\\text{fast}} t),\\quad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{slow}})| \\approx 1,\\quad |H(j\\omega_{\\text{fast}})| \\approx 0.1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the low-pass rule to each component: the slow cosine keeps nearly full size, while the fast cosine is multiplied by a much smaller factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t) \\approx \\cos(\\omega_{\\text{slow}} t) + 0.1\\,\\cos(\\omega_{\\text{fast}} t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the output is dominated by the slow variation, and the rapid variation is still present but strongly suppressed. That is the low-pass pattern in sinusoidal form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
